## 9. Bulk Purchase Management

This feature is different from multiple independent daily expenses.

It is for recording a single large shopping bill containing multiple purchased items.

Example:

The owner purchases the following from a wholesale market:

| Item | Quantity | Rate | Total |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Rice | 25 kg | ₹50 | ₹1,250 |
| Cooking Oil | 10 litre | ₹140 | ₹1,400 |
| Potatoes | 20 kg | ₹25 | ₹500 |
| Onions | 15 kg | ₹30 | ₹450 |
| Spices | 1 lot | ₹600 | ₹600 |

### Bulk purchase form

Bill details:
- Purchase Date.
- Vendor / Supplier Name.
- Invoice Number.
- Payment Method.
- Payment Status.
- Notes.
- Optional Bill Photo.

### Purchase line items

Each item must contain:

- Item Name.
- Category.
- Subcategory.
- Quantity.
- Unit.
- Unit Price.
- Discount (optional).
- Tax (optional).
- Final Item Amount.

Allow the owner to add or remove as many item rows as necessary.

### Automatic calculations

Calculate:

- Subtotal.
- Total Discount.
- Total Tax.
- Additional Delivery / Transport Charges.
- Final Bill Total.

If invoice-level discounts, tax, or extra charges are applied, allocate them proportionally across the line items for accurate category reporting.

Ensure that the sum of all saved line-item amounts is exactly equal to the final bill total.

### Saving behaviour

- Give every bulk purchase a unique purchase group ID.
- Save individual line items with that group ID.
- Show the purchase as one grouped bill in the interface.
- Let the owner open the bill to see all its items.
- Editing a bill must update its associated line items correctly.
- Deleting a bill must not leave orphaned items.
- Never count both the bulk purchase total and its individual items as separate expenses.

The report calculations must count each rupee exactly once.

